{
  "id": "passiv",
  "name": "The passive: all tenses, modals, it is said that",
  "level": "B2",
  "subtitle": "In the passive, what happens matters more than who does it. Use be + past participle in every tense, and change only be: The room is being cleaned. The report has been sent. The hotel must be booked today.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "be + past participle",
    "is being cleaned",
    "has been sent",
    "must be booked"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-b2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "Subject (= object of the active sentence)"
      },
      {
        "text": "be in the right tense · modal + be",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "past participle",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "They are repairing the road. → The road <span class=\"vp-verb\">is being</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">repaired</span>. — You must book the hotel. → The hotel <span class=\"vp-verb\">must be</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">booked</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>The passive is be + past participle.</strong> The tense is in <em>be</em>. The participle never changes. <em>The room is cleaned. The room was cleaned. The room will be cleaned.</em> The object of the active sentence becomes the subject: <em>They clean <strong>the room</strong></em> → <em><strong>The room</strong> is cleaned.</em>",
        "tag": "All tenses"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>being</strong> is for an action in progress: <em>The road is being repaired</em> (right now). <em>The tables were being laid when we arrived.</em> <strong>been</strong> is for the present perfect: <em>The road has been repaired</em> (it is finished). <em>The invitations have been sent.</em>",
        "tag": "being and been"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>With a modal verb</strong> use modal + <em>be</em> + past participle: <em>The report must be finished today. This watch can't be repaired.</em> For the past, use modal + <em>have been</em> + past participle: <em>The letter should have been sent yesterday.</em>",
        "tag": "With modals"
      },
      {
        "html": "After <em>want, need, hope</em> use <strong>to be + past participle</strong>: <em>She wants to be told the truth.</em> After <em>like, hate, enjoy</em> use <strong>being + past participle</strong>: <em>He hates being interrupted.</em> <em>Nobody likes being laughed at.</em>",
        "tag": "to be done, being done"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>It is said that … / He is said to …</strong> is a formal way to share news and opinions. <em>It is said that the bridge is 200 years old.</em> = <em>The bridge is said to be 200 years old.</em> Use it with <em>said, believed, thought, known, expected, reported</em>. For an earlier time use <em>to have</em> + past participle: <em>The thief is thought to have left the country.</em>",
        "tag": "It is said that …"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>The bridge is repairing now.</em> → ✓ <em>The bridge is being repaired now.</em> · ✗ <em>The parcels have sent.</em> → ✓ <em>The parcels have been sent.</em> · ✗ <em>It was happened last night.</em> → ✓ <em>It happened last night.</em> (<em>happen</em> has no object, so it has no passive)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Use the passive when the doer is unknown, not important or obvious: <em>My bike has been stolen.</em> Add <em>by</em> only when the doer is new or interesting information: <em>The song was written by a ten-year-old.</em> Verbs without an object (<em>happen, arrive, die, seem</em>) have no passive. For the basics (present and past simple, <em>by</em>), see <a href=\"/passive-basics\">Passive basics</a>. To say that someone else does a job for you, see <a href=\"/have-something-done\">Have something done</a>. For modal verbs, see <a href=\"/modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</a>.",
